What is Static CALL and Dynamic CALL?
Static CALL
A Static CALL is resolved during the program's build/link-edit process. The called program is linked with the calling program.

Syntax:
CALL "SUBPGM".

With IBM Enterprise COBOL, a literal CALL is generally static under the NODYNAM compiler option.


Dynamic CALL
A Dynamic CALL loads or resolves the called program at execution time.

Syntax:
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.

       01 WS-PGM-NAME PIC X(8)
          VALUE "SUBPGM".

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           CALL WS-PGM-NAME.

           STOP RUN.


A CALL using an identifier is dynamic in normal Enterprise COBOL usage.
Static vs Dynamic
Static CALL	Dynamic CALL
Resolved at build time	Resolved at execution time
Called program linked with caller	Called program loaded separately
Generally uses more linked executable space	Called module can be shared/reused
Literal CALL with NODYNAM	Identifier CALL or DYNAM option
